# Why Reliable Detectors Still Find Impostors

Open index.html directly, or serve it alongside TeachingTools. The survey,
Bayesian calculation and Canvas plots run locally without R, Shiny, WebR,
external assets or data upload. Existing app.R/model.R are unchanged references.

Lecture: https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section2b_html.html

## Model and interpretation

For each of 10,000 independent objects, the real-event probability is
q=10^log_prevalence. Real events are flagged with sensitivity s; non-events
with false-positive probability f. These probabilities are known inputs.
The theoretical positive predictive value is q*s / [q*s+(1-q)*f], not s.

One mosaic square is one actually flagged object. Expected and realised
true/false counts are separate in a stacked-bar comparison and outcome table.
Expected counts may be fractional and are never rounded into pretend mosaic
objects. The Bayesian probability uses full-precision probabilities, not the
displayed, rounded counts or the observed genuine fraction. Rare probabilities
are shown with significant figures rather than forced to 0.0%.

An empty survey has no realised genuine fraction; it does not imply zero PPV.
If the probability model itself assigns zero probability to all flags, PPV is
undefined (null in the pure model API). Hiding truth suppresses all
truth-dependent realised metrics/bars as well as candidate colours.

## Intentional differences from R

- R defaults, assumptions and guided rare/common cases are retained.
- log10 prevalence extends from -4 down to -6, and the false-positive rate
  permits zero, exposing rare/empty limits rather than hiding them.
- Expected/realised outcome comparisons and a zero-false-positive preset are
  added. Undefined observed fractions are explicitly labelled.
- Astro.createRng(seed), not R's stream, generates fixed survey-wide truth and
  flag draws. Playback reveals a cached survey in 100 percentage steps rather
  than 60. Hiding truth, playback and resizing cannot change the data.
- Seed validation supports 0..2147483647.

## Files and exports

Runtime: index.html, model.js, app.js, plus shared statistics.js, browser.js,
lesson.js and browser.css in TeachingTools. No additional runtime assets.
Pure CommonJS/browser global BaseRatesModel exports positivePredictiveValue,
expectedCounts, compute and reveal. compute parameters match form IDs:
seed, log_prevalence, sensitivity, false_positive, reveal.
reveal(result, percentage=100) returns actual/expected confusion counts,
scanned objects, candidate indices and an observed genuineFraction or null.
Expected invalid inputs throw Astro.ValidationError.

## Validation

From the repository root, with Node 22+ and Chrome:

    node BaseRates/test_model.js
    mkdir -p BaseRates/.browser-profile
    env -u HTML_SITE_ROOT TMPDIR="$PWD/BaseRates/.browser-profile" node BaseRates/test_browser.js
    rmdir BaseRates/.browser-profile

Numerical tests check retained R PPVs (0.48969072164948452 at q=.01,
s=.95,f=.01; 0.0094115316029324356 at q=.0001), independent natural
frequencies 950/(950+9990), fractional expectations, empty/zero-probability
limits, explicit confusion counts, identity-preserving display controls,
cached partial surveys and invalid inputs.

    Rscript -e 'source("BaseRates/model.R"); print(positive_predictive_value(.0001,.95,.01),digits=17)'

Browser tests assert actual coloured mosaic pixels and metrics, hidden truth,
rare nonzero PPV, expected-versus-realised meaning, a no-flags run with
theoretical PPV=1, cached animation, plus the shared presets/reset, seed
validation, keyboard tabs, mobile and offline-file checks.
